1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,630
Imagine you are purchasing a brand-new smartphone priced at twelve million dong.

2
00:00:03,630 --> 00:00:10,750
On the counter, a bold promotional brochure advertises zero-percent installment financing, split evenly across twelve monthly cycles at exactly one million dong per month.

3
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:12,960
You sign your name, collect the device, and walk out.

4
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:16,120
On the surface, nobody appears to be charging you a single coin of interest.

5
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:19,620
But in commercial reality, no institution lends capital for an entire year for free.

6
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:21,740
So who is quietly paying the interest on this loan?

7
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:29,900
Over the next eight minutes, we trace every electronic message, every ledger journal, and every wholesale bank transfer to uncover the six hundred thousand dong resting quietly behind the counter.

8
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:35,890
On a Saturday afternoon, in this working example, you stand at the counter of a consumer electronics store.

9
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:43,870
The smartphone you want costs twelve million dong, accompanied by a promotional brochure promising zero percent financing across twelve monthly cycles at one million dong each.

10
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:49,950
The sales associate takes a snapshot of your identity card, enters your personal details into an underwriting tablet, and taps submit.

11
00:00:49,950 --> 00:00:54,580
Look closely at the information pathway: what was just dispatched is purely an electronic data packet.

12
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:56,440
It carries absolutely zero physical cash.

13
00:00:56,440 --> 00:01:01,610
And notice where that message travels: not to the store's retail bank, but directly to an independent third party.

14
00:01:01,610 --> 00:01:06,240
Keep this question in mind: if nobody is charging you interest, who pays for twelve months of waiting?

15
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:09,750
That third party is a consumer finance company.

16
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:16,890
Their automated algorithms review your application, score your credit profile, and fifteen minutes later transmit an approval confirmation.

17
00:01:16,890 --> 00:01:23,970
A digital loan agreement is executed directly between you and the finance company: that is the promissory debt commitment you just signed.

18
00:01:23,970 --> 00:01:32,080
Because a loan contract represents an agreement to pay future funds, at this exact second only legal information has been recorded, with zero funds disbursed.

19
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:38,960
From this moment onward, the institution you legally owe twelve million dong to is the consumer finance company, not the retail store.

20
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,730
If you are late on a payment, they assess the penalty.

21
00:01:41,730 --> 00:01:43,480
The store simply sold merchandise.

22
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:48,820
All collection overhead, non-payment risk, and borrower servicing shift entirely to the finance company.

23
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:51,450
You walk home with the smartphone.

24
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:54,690
That evening, the retail store closes its daily trading books.

25
00:01:54,690 --> 00:01:59,130
If you were to open the physical cash drawer, you would not find twelve million dong.

26
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:03,460
If you checked the store's corporate banking portal, no customer funds have landed.

27
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:07,740
No wholesale wire transfer has executed yet, and the bank has not disbursed money.

28
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:09,200
Not a single coin has moved.

29
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,280
So tonight, where does the money for that smartphone sleep?

30
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:14,580
It sleeps inside an accounting ledger entry.

31
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:22,260
The store's accountant records a receivable of twelve million dong, specifying clearly: receivable from the consumer finance company, not from you.

32
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:29,210
In corporate accounting charts, that line sits in Account 1388 under Other Receivables, rather than Account 131 for retail customers.

33
00:02:29,210 --> 00:02:33,020
Meanwhile, you possess the phone without having parted with upfront cash.

34
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:34,580
That is the essence of a loan.

35
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:41,120
The following morning, assuming a standard business day, the interbank disbursement wire executes.

36
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:47,430
Real wholesale cash transfers from the finance company to the store's bank, and on into the store's commercial account.

37
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:51,030
But inspect the credited sum closely: it is not twelve million dong.

38
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:54,850
The actual deposit is exactly eleven million four hundred thousand dong.

39
00:02:54,850 --> 00:02:59,620
Six hundred thousand dong was withheld at source by the finance company during settlement.

40
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:06,620
That missing six hundred thousand dong represents a five percent interest subsidy that the retail store quietly paid on your behalf.

41
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:18,760
In its accounting records, the store enters Debit Account 112 for eleven million four hundred thousand, Debit Account 641 for selling expenses of six hundred thousand, and balances Credit Account 1388 for the full twelve million.

42
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:21,040
Yesterday's written promise clears to zero.

43
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:32,790
The store accepts a five percent haircut on its retail tag in exchange for locking in an immediate sale, receiving cleared liquidity after one business day, and passing one hundred percent of collection risk to the lender.

44
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:38,700
Now observe the consumer finance company's balance sheet over the following twelve months.

45
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:44,630
Every month, an automated debit mandate deducts one million dong from your checking account and remits it to the lender.

46
00:03:44,630 --> 00:03:48,290
Over twelve billing cycles, they collect a cumulative twelve million dong.

47
00:03:48,290 --> 00:03:56,150
Notice the macro structure: the finance company disbursed eleven million four hundred thousand dong on day one and reclaimed twelve million dong over one year.

48
00:03:56,150 --> 00:03:59,860
The six hundred thousand dong spread represents their earned lending yield.

49
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:14,600
When calculated on a declining principal balance across twelve months, an initial outlay of eleven point four million yielding one million monthly equates to an internal rate of return of approximately zero point eight percent per month, or an effective annual rate of roughly ten percent per year.

50
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:22,810
From the finance company's perspective, this is a standard interest-bearing credit portfolio earning ten percent annually, funded entirely by the merchant's discount.

51
00:04:23,990 --> 00:04:30,220
Now examine the critical commercial question: who truly pays that six hundred thousand dong interest charge?

52
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:33,390
The answer depends entirely on upfront pricing reality.

53
00:04:33,390 --> 00:04:39,850
Imagine you ask the store manager: if I pay with liquid cash right now in a single payment, does the price drop?

54
00:04:39,850 --> 00:04:54,680
If the store offers a cash discount of six hundred thousand dong, bringing the upfront price to eleven million four hundred thousand, then by selecting the installment plan at twelve million, you personally paid that six hundred thousand dong financing fee.

55
00:04:54,680 --> 00:05:00,220
You surrendered a tangible cash discount in exchange for stretching payments over twelve months.

56
00:05:00,220 --> 00:05:04,430
The flyer did not breach legal terms: your contract rate is zero percent.

57
00:05:04,430 --> 00:05:10,890
But because the cash alternative was cheaper, your effective borrowing cost is zero point eight percent monthly.

58
00:05:10,890 --> 00:05:15,910
If you take the phone home today for twelve million, that spread was the price of time.

59
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:28,280
What happens in the alternative scenario, where the retailer enforces an identical twelve-million-dong price tag whether you pay upfront or finance over twelve months?

60
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:37,960
In that situation, you genuinely enjoy zero percent interest, and the six hundred thousand dong financing subsidy is absorbed entirely by the merchant.

61
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:42,250
Why would a retail business forfeit five percent of gross turnover?

62
00:05:42,250 --> 00:05:52,950
Because prominent promotional signs advertising zero-percent financing attract substantial foot traffic into stores, converting hesitant browsers into verified buyers.

63
00:05:52,950 --> 00:06:06,730
For the retailer, surrendering five percent of gross margin is far more economical than allowing valuable electronics inventory to sit unsold on display shelves for extra weeks while bearing warehouse holding costs.

64
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:21,110
There is another common financing route: swiping a personal credit card for twelve million dong at checkout and subsequently requesting your card issuer to convert the transaction into twelve monthly installments.

65
00:06:21,110 --> 00:06:27,120
In that model, your creditor is the card issuing bank rather than an independent finance company.

66
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:35,970
While marketed as zero-percent interest, card issuers frequently assess a mandatory installment conversion fee of around three percent upfront.

67
00:06:35,970 --> 00:06:42,970
On a twelve-million-dong purchase, that one-time conversion fee amounts to three hundred and sixty thousand dong.

68
00:06:42,970 --> 00:06:59,870
When amortized across twelve months of declining principal balances, that upfront administrative fee translates to an effective annual borrowing rate of roughly five point six to six point two percent, paid directly by the cardholder rather than subsidized by the retailer.

69
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:05,050
Now consider what occurs when operational plans experience disruption.

70
00:07:05,050 --> 00:07:10,030
Scenario one: suppose you decide to settle the entire debt early at the end of month four.

71
00:07:10,030 --> 00:07:18,340
Because the nominal contract rate is zero percent, your remaining principal equals exactly eight outstanding installments totaling eight million dong.

72
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:23,930
Prepaying early yields zero interest savings, and the contract may even levy an early settlement fee.

73
00:07:23,930 --> 00:07:31,630
Compressing that upfront subsidy across four months elevates your effective monthly borrowing cost to roughly one point four eight percent.

74
00:07:31,630 --> 00:07:40,990
Scenario two: if you miss a scheduled installment payment, penalty fees and default interest accelerate borrowing costs while generating an adverse credit bureau record.

75
00:07:40,990 --> 00:07:51,780
Scenario three: if you return the device to the store, the sales agreement is cancelled, but your debt obligation only closes once the merchant refunds wholesale cash back to the finance company.

76
00:07:53,010 --> 00:07:59,810
In the final analysis, promotional flyers do not deceive: the interest rate printed on your contract is legally zero percent.

77
00:07:59,810 --> 00:08:03,340
However, economic interest never vanishes from financial reality.

78
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:08,230
It merely vacates the visible interest line and hides within the retail transaction price.

79
00:08:08,230 --> 00:08:15,680
The six hundred thousand dong spread between the store's net proceeds and cumulative loan payments represents genuine financing interest.

80
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:24,220
The next time you encounter a bold zero-percent financing banner, ask the store clerk one simple question: what is the cash price if I pay in full right now?

81
00:08:24,220 --> 00:08:27,430
The difference that emerges is the real price of borrowing.
